steps.body:
An order gateway receives every message as a `Message&`, but a cancel has to reach the order it cancels. `dynamic_cast` asks, while the program runs, what an object really is:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

class Message {
public:
    virtual ~Message() = default;
};

class Cancel : public Message {
public:
    int id() const { return 7; }
};

void handle(const Message& msg) {
    {{code}}
}

int main() {
    handle(Cancel());
    handle(Message());
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
if (const auto* cancel = dynamic_cast<const Cancel*>(&msg)) {
    std::cout << "cancel " << cancel->id() << '\n';
} else {
    std::cout << "not a cancel\n";
}
```

- On a pointer it returns `nullptr` when the object is neither a `Cancel` nor derived from one. `if (const auto* cancel = ...)` declares the pointer and tests it in one line.
- On a reference it can't return null, so it throws `std::bad_cast` from `<typeinfo>` instead.
- It needs a **polymorphic** class, one with at least one virtual function. The hidden vtable pointer in each object leads to its class's **RTTI**, run-time type information: a record of the class and its bases, which `dynamic_cast` checks. `typeid(msg).name()` reads the same record and gives a mangled name such as `6Cancel` with g++ and clang.

`static_cast<const Cancel&>(msg)` compiles too, but checks nothing, so it is undefined behavior when `msg` is something else. Use it only when you know the type for certain, as CRTP does in the advanced *Templates, Virtual Calls, `std::function`, and `std::variant`* lesson. With the interfaces of *Multiple Inheritance, the Diamond, and Virtual Bases*, `dynamic_cast` can also cast across from one base to another.

The check has a cost. On this Mac at `-O2`, one `dynamic_cast` took about 12 ns against about 1 ns for a virtual call, rough numbers. Some low-latency codebases build with `-fno-rtti` to drop the records, and then this Mac's clang refuses the cast:

```text
$ clang++ -std=c++17 -fno-rtti -c nortti.cpp
nortti.cpp:3:42: error: use of dynamic_cast requires -frtti
    3 | bool isCancel(const Message& m) { return dynamic_cast<const Cancel*>(&m) != nullptr; }
      |                                          ^
1 error generated.
```

A chain of `dynamic_cast` tests often means a virtual function is missing: give `Message` a `virtual void apply(OrderBook& book)` and let each class do its own work.

